# How git should treat the bytes in this repository.
#
# Three jobs: keep line endings from becoming a diff, keep generated files out
# of review and out of GitHub's language stats, and give git the hunk-header
# rules that make a diff readable.
#
# NOT here on purpose: `export-ignore`. It would trim files out of the tarball
# GitHub generates for a tag, and that tarball is what a downstream `zig fetch`
# hashes - the url+hash pin in a consumer's `build.zig.zon` is a hash of those
# bytes. Excluding `bench/` to save someone a download would invalidate every
# pin that already exists. What ships in the Zig package is already decided,
# deliberately and reviewably, by `.paths` in build.zig.zon.

# ── Line endings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Normalize on commit, LF in the working tree on every platform - including
# Windows, which this project builds and tests natively. The parity suite
# compares gist's bytes against ripgrep's, so a checkout that silently
# introduced CRLF would change the thing under test and fail the comparison for
# a reason that has nothing to do with either tool.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one exception, and it is a real one: a batch file is read by cmd.exe,
# which has historically wanted CRLF. `install.ps1` does not - PowerShell is
# fine with LF - so it stays under the rule above.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# ── Binary ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The README figures under assets/.
*.png binary

# ── Generated ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Vim's help index, written by `:helptags`. Never edited, and a diff of it says
# nothing a diff of `gist.txt` does not say better.
editor/vim/doc/tags  linguist-generated -diff

# Lockfiles: resolver output. Reviewed as a fact ("this version moved"), never
# read line by line.
*.lock  linguist-generated -diff

# Anything projected from contract/*.toml, should the sync ever land its output
# in the tree rather than checking it. Nothing matches today; the rule is here
# so that the day one does, the diff worth reading is still the contract's.
*.gen.zig      linguist-generated
schema.gen.*   linguist-generated

# NOT marked, because they never reach the tree: the manual and the shell
# completions are rendered at install time by `gist --generate` from the same
# flag catalog argv is parsed with. That is why a flag cannot exist in the
# parser and be missing from a menu.

# ── Diff drivers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Git ships hunk-header patterns for these; naming them here is what makes
# `@@ ... fn whatever` appear above a hunk instead of a bare line number.
*.go  diff=golang
*.py  diff=python
*.rs  diff=rust
*.h   diff=cpp
*.md  diff=markdown

# Zig has no built-in driver. The attribute is inert until the pattern exists
# in your own git config; CONTRIBUTING.md has the one-line command, and without
# it this line simply falls back to git's default behaviour.
*.zig diff=zig

# ── Merge ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The changelog is append-at-the-top and assembled by towncrier. Two branches
# adding release sections conflict textually while agreeing semantically; a
# union merge keeps both and lets the next `towncrier build` sort it out.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
